/**
@page porting Porting to other hardware

@brief What a third target needs - a new HAL implementation behind the same
       interface.

Everything platform-specific in Botanik lives behind one seam: the Hardware
Abstraction Layer (`src/libbotanik/hal/`). The rest of the codebase calls only
`HAL::` functions and never touches `<time.h>`, Mbed, or board APIs directly. So a
new target is, structurally, one new HAL implementation - no subsystem changes.

@section porting_seam The seam

`hal/platform.h` declares the interface and selects an implementation at compile
time:

@code{.cpp}
#ifdef TARGET_OPTA
    #include "libbotanik/hal/opta.h"
#else
    #include "libbotanik/hal/simulator.h"
#endif
@endcode

A new target adds `hal/<target>.h` implementing every declared function, a
`TARGET_<NAME>` define, and a branch here (plus a CMake toolchain entry). Use
`simulator.h` and `opta.h` as worked references: the simulator gives the "no real
hardware" answers (barriers/cache ops as no-ops, time from `std::chrono`), the OPTA the
device answers.

@section porting_surface What to implement

The interface groups into a handful of concerns:

| Concern | Functions | Notes |
|---------|-----------|-------|
| **Time** | `current_ms`, `uptime_ms`, `wall_clock_ms`, `wall_clock_set`, `sleep_ms` | A monotonic millisecond clock is the only hard requirement; wall-clock may be a stub. |
| **Memory ordering** | `dsb`, `dmb`, `ordered_copy`, `ordered_move`, `ordered_read` | Real barriers on a multi-core/cached target; no-ops where there is one core and no cache. The PLC sync correctness rests on these. |
| **Cache maintenance** | `cache_clean`, `cache_invalidate` | Required if the PLC shared region is cacheable; cheap no-ops otherwise. |
| **PLC shared memory** | `get_plc_input`, `get_plc_output` | Return the base of each shared region. On a target with a real coprocessor these point at the shared SRAM; a simulator-style target allocates them locally. |
| **Watchdog** | `watchdog_start`, `watchdog_kick`, `watchdog_stop` | The hardware watchdog @ref Botanik::Health::HealthMonitor escalates to. |
| **Stack introspection** | `get_stack_base`, `get_stack_depth`, `get_stack_high` | Feeds the perf monitor; the high-water figure may return 0 if unavailable. |
| **Debug / lifecycle** | `debug_break`, `under_debugger`, `reset`, `hardware_description` | `under_debugger` gates `debug_break` so a bare trap never faults a debugger-less unit. |

@section porting_check Validating a port

The simulator and the `OPTA` ARM build already exercise the two ends of the
spectrum; a new HAL is correct when the existing subsystems run unchanged on top of
it. Bring it up against the @ref reference "reference site" and watch the boot:
@ref md_docs_2POST "POST" validates the shared-region pointers and alignment, so a
mis-wired `get_plc_*` shows up at once as a critical on the `BOOT` topic.
*/
